.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"

*******************************************************************************
* SUBCIRCUIT 1: fa (full adder)
*
*   We'll define p = a XOR b so:
*   sum = p XOR carry_in
*   Then De Morgan to build the OR out of NANDs:
*   carry = ab + p.carry_in = !( !(ab) . !(p.carry_in) )
*
*   Gate count: 5 (two XOR2, three NAND2)
*
*   Why we use XORs (built-in):
*   Using XORs is much more efficient and simple, because it would only use 5 gates.
*   Other solutions would require more:
*   - A NAND-built XOR would require four NAND gates, so the two XORs become eight
*   - An all-NAND or all-NOR full adder would require nine gates 
*   
*   According to ./stdcell.jsim though, a xor2 gate's size and propagation delay
*   are 27 and 140ps respectively, while nand2's are 10 and 30ps; this means that
*   a full-NAND circuit is actually faster than what we have, even though it uses more 
*   gates. This is a noted tradeoff.
*
*   Moreover, we still use NANDs for the carry, because the carry is a plain AND-OR expression. 
*
*******************************************************************************
.subckt fa a b carry_in sum carry
    Xh1 a b p xor2             
    Xh2 p carry_in sum xor2    
    Xh3 a b nab nand2          
    Xh4 p carry_in npc nand2    
    Xh5 nab npc carry nand2    
.ends

*******************************************************************************
* SUBCIRCUIT 2: add32 (32-bit ripple carry adder)
*
*   In one line, we chain 32 of our fa's, one per bit, each passing its carry up to the next.
*
*   Instead of writing 32 lines, we use JSim's iterated instantiation. If we hand 
*   a device more signals than it has ports, JSim flattens the signal list, cuts it into as many chunks 
*   as there are ports. It then makes one copy per slot, taking the i-th signal of every chunk.
*   fa has 5 ports and we give it 160 signals, so we get 160/5 = 32 copies.
*   Take note that a[31:0] expands as a31 down to a0, so copy #0 is bit 31 (the MSB) and copy #31 is bit 0 (the LSB).
*
*   The carry_in chunk is "c[30:0] cin0" and the carry chunk is "carry_out c[30:0]". Both are 32 signals, but
*   the c's sit one slot apart, so the two chunks line up staggered and every fa's carry lands on
*   the same wire the next fa reads as its carry_in. That offset lets us wire the ripple.
*
*   Gate count: 160 (32 fa's, so 64 XOR2 and 96 NAND2)
*
*   Delay explaination:
*   Since each fa has to wait for the one below it, in the worst case the carry walks the entire chain. 
*   According to ./stdcell.jsim, carry_in to carry costs two nand2's, so 60ps per stage, and 32 stages is roughly
*   2ns. We see this in test cases i and j (0x00000001 + 0xFFFFFFFF), where only bit 0 makes its own
*   carry and every bit above it merely passes it along, so it takes about 2ns to
*   settle versus about 0.3ns for the other cases. A ripple carry adder's delay grows with size, and a carry 
*   lookahead adder (the better alternative) exists to address that issue; we chose to build the former as
*   specified in the specs (it's also the simplest).
*   
*******************************************************************************
.subckt add32 a[31:0] b[31:0] cin0 sum[31:0] carry_out
    Xf a[31:0] b[31:0] c[30:0] cin0 sum[31:0] carry_out c[30:0] fa
.ends



*******************************************************************************
* INSTANTIATIONS
*******************************************************************************

Xsum a[31:0] b[31:0] 0 sum[31:0] carry_out add32


*******************************************************************************
* SIMULATION
*******************************************************************************

Wa a[31:0] nrz(0, 5, 20ns, 0ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns) 0x00000000 0x55555555 0x00000000 0x55555555 0xFFFFFFFF 0xAAAAAAAA 0x00000000 0xAAAAAAAA 0x00000001 0xFFFFFFFF
Wb b[31:0] nrz(0, 5, 20ns, 0ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns) 0x00000000 0x00000000 0x55555555 0x55555555 0xFFFFFFFF 0x00000000 0xAAAAAAAA 0xAAAAAAAA 0xFFFFFFFF 0x00000001
.tran 220ns
.plot a[31:0] 
.plot b[31:0]
.plot sum[31:0] 
.plot carry_out
